import{Q as e,X as t,ft as n,n as r}from"./chunks/framework.BK-VYr1i.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Investigate macOS Applications","description":"","frontmatter":{"id":"guide.macos-application-investigation","title":"Investigate macOS Applications","describes":["proposal.0001","proposal.0003","proposal.0004"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guides/macos-application-investigation.md","filePath":"guides/macos-application-investigation.md"}`),a={name:`guides/macos-application-investigation.md`};function o(r,i,a,o,s,c){return n(),t(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<h1 id="investigate-macos-applications" tabindex="-1">Investigate macOS Applications <a class="header-anchor" href="#investigate-macos-applications" aria-label="Permalink to “Investigate macOS Applications”">​</a></h1><h2 id="what-it-does" tabindex="-1">What it does <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-it-does" aria-label="Permalink to “What it does”">​</a></h2><p>Recon can list application bundles known to macOS, inspect one bundle and its running process IDs, and launch a selected bundle. <code>list</code> and <code>inspect</code> read metadata without opening an application, changing the foreground app, moving the pointer, or sending input. <code>launch</code> is an explicit action that can bring the selected app to the foreground. These commands do not inspect windows, controls, screenshots, or executable code.</p><h2 id="how-to-use-it" tabindex="-1">How to use it <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-to-use-it" aria-label="Permalink to “How to use it”">​</a></h2><p>On a Mac with the <code>recon</code> executable installed, run <code>recon app list</code> to obtain a JSON object with <code>discovery: &quot;indexed-and-running&quot;</code> and an <code>applications</code> array. Each entry contains a displayed <code>name</code>, a <code>bundleId</code> that may be <code>null</code>, an absolute bundle <code>path</code>, and <code>runningPids</code>. The list combines indexed application bundles with running apps that expose a bundle location. It is not an exhaustive inventory of every app on every volume. A running app without an identifiable bundle location cannot appear in this list.</p><p>Run <code>recon app list --running</code> to ask the narrower question: which of those applications is running right now. The result holds exactly the entries whose <code>runningPids</code> is not empty, with the same fields, the same order (name, then path), and the same ascending process IDs as the full list, and its <code>discovery</code> reads <code>&quot;running&quot;</code> rather than <code>&quot;indexed-and-running&quot;</code>, so a result you keep says which question it answers. When no application is running, <code>applications</code> is an empty array and the command still succeeds. <code>--running</code> takes no value and may appear once; <code>--running=yes</code>, a repeated <code>--running</code>, a value after it, or a target beside it is a usage error, and <code>inspect</code> and <code>launch</code> do not accept it.</p><p>Run <code>recon app inspect &lt;target&gt;</code> for details of one app. A target can be an exact displayed name, an exact bundle identifier (matched without regard to case), or an absolute <code>.app</code> path. An absolute path also works for a valid app that the list did not discover. If a name or identifier matches multiple locations, Recon fails with the code <code>AMBIGUOUS_TARGET</code>, whose <code>details.candidates</code> lists every matching bundle by <code>name</code>, <code>bundleId</code>, and <code>path</code>; pass the path of the copy you intend to inspect. If a string matches both an app name and another app&#39;s identifier, it is ambiguous rather than favoring either one.</p><p>Inspection returns an <code>application</code> object with <code>name</code>, <code>bundleId</code>, <code>path</code>, <code>version</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>executablePath</code>, and <code>runningPids</code>. The bundle identifier, version, build, or executable path may be <code>null</code> when unavailable. The process IDs describe instances observed at the time of inspection; an empty array is not a promise that the app will remain stopped. The app&#39;s displayed name falls back to its bundle filename when macOS supplies no display name. Neither inspecting nor listing changes the foreground app.</p><p>Run <code>recon app launch &lt;target&gt;</code> only when you intend to open or activate the selected app. It uses the same targeting rules, does not request a second instance, and does not silently substitute another installed copy. On success the JSON result contains the selected bundle <code>path</code> and the host-reported <code>pid</code>. Success means macOS opened or activated the app; it does not mean a window or control is ready.</p><p>Every command prints one JSON object to standard output and exits 0 on success, or one JSON error object on a single line to standard error and exits nonzero on failure. The error object is <code>{&quot;error&quot;:{&quot;code&quot;:…,&quot;message&quot;:…,&quot;retryable&quot;:…,&quot;command&quot;:[…],&quot;details&quot;:{…}}}</code>. Branch on <code>code</code>, which comes from a closed set; read <code>details</code> for the values you need to recover, such as the candidate bundles of an ambiguity or the reason a path is not an application. <code>retryable</code> is <code>true</code> only when repeating the identical command could succeed on its own.</p><p>To learn the commands without reading this repository, run <code>recon --help</code> for the command list, <code>recon schema app inspect</code> for one command&#39;s arguments, output schema, and error codes, and <code>recon context</code> for this installation&#39;s version, permissions, and whether application discovery works here. The <a href="./cli-contract">CLI contract guide</a> covers orientation, <code>recon schema</code>, <code>recon context</code>, and how to read an error object.</p><h2 id="worked-example" tabindex="-1">Worked example <a class="header-anchor" href="#worked-example" aria-label="Permalink to “Worked example”">​</a></h2><p>To see which applications are running before choosing one, run:</p><div class="language-sh"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">recon</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> app</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> list</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;--shiki-light:#005CC5;"> --running</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Every entry in <code>applications</code> is an application with at least one process ID observed at that moment, and <code>discovery</code> reads <code>&quot;running&quot;</code>. Nothing is opened, activated, or quit.</p><p>On a Mac with the built-in Calculator app, first inspect it without opening it:</p><div class="language-sh"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">recon</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> app</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> inspect</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> /System/Applications/Calculator.app</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The JSON result identifies the Calculator bundle and reports any running process IDs seen at that moment. To open it deliberately, run:</p><div class="language-sh"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">recon</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> app</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> launch</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> /System/Applications/Calculator.app</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This second command may foreground Calculator. If you only need to identify the app, stop after <code>inspect</code>.</p><h2 id="limits-and-edge-cases" tabindex="-1">Limits and edge cases <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits-and-edge-cases" aria-label="Permalink to “Limits and edge cases”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Discovery relies on the host&#39;s indexed and accessible locations plus running apps with bundle locations. If indexing is unavailable, <code>list</code> fails with <code>DISCOVERY_UNAVAILABLE</code> instead of returning a misleading partial inventory; that code is retryable, because Spotlight may answer a later attempt. Explicit app paths remain usable.</li><li><code>--running</code> narrows the list, not the discovery behind it. Recon still consults both the index and the running applications, so an application running from a bundle the index does not list is still reported, and an unavailable index is still <code>DISCOVERY_UNAVAILABLE</code> rather than a list of running applications alone.</li><li>Every failure prints one JSON error object to standard error, exits nonzero, and prints no success JSON. A path that cannot be read is <code>NOT_FOUND</code> with <code>details.kind: &quot;path&quot;</code>. A path that exists but is not a readable application bundle is <code>NOT_AN_APPLICATION</code>, with <code>details.reason</code> of <code>not-a-bundle-directory</code>, <code>unreadable-info-plist</code>, or <code>no-name</code>. A selector that matches nothing is <code>NOT_FOUND</code> with <code>details.kind: &quot;application&quot;</code> and the selector you passed. A selector that matches several bundles is <code>AMBIGUOUS_TARGET</code>. A refused launch is <code>LAUNCH_FAILED</code> with <code>details.reason</code>.</li><li>Recon treats a directory as an application when its extension is <code>.app</code>, in any case, and it contains a readable <code>Contents/Info.plist</code> dictionary. As with macOS, the bundle&#39;s declared package type does not matter, so system apps such as Finder and Passwords resolve like any other app.</li><li>If a bundle has no executable file, <code>launch</code> fails with <code>LAUNCH_FAILED</code> and <code>details.reason: &quot;executable-missing&quot;</code> before asking macOS to open it; the message still reads <code>application executable is missing</code>.</li><li>Process IDs can disappear or be reused after the command returns. Use the app&#39;s absolute bundle path when referring to a particular installed copy.</li><li>On a non-macOS host these commands fail with <code>UNSUPPORTED_HOST</code>, whose <code>details</code> name the host you are on and the hosts the command supports; they do not return an empty success result.</li><li>These commands need no Accessibility or Screen Recording grant for their bundle and process metadata. Launch can still encounter ordinary macOS security prompts or errors; Recon does not bypass them.</li></ul>`,22)]])}var s=r(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};